The main argument for street trees is the comfort and health of residents. Trees give shade, so pavements and nearby homes stay cooler during hot summers. Their leaves also trap dust from traffic, which makes the air cleaner for people walking to school or work. A street with trees feels calmer, and people are more likely to walk or cycle along it.

Fewer parking spaces would also encourage healthier habits. If drivers cannot park easily in the city centre, more of them will take the bus or use a bicycle. This reduces traffic and noise, which benefits everyone, including the drivers who still need to use their cars.

However, removing parking suddenly could cause problems. Shops depend on customers who arrive by car, and some people, such as elderly residents or workers carrying tools, have no realistic alternative. Cities should therefore remove parking in stages, starting with streets that already have good public transport, and keep spaces for disabled drivers and deliveries.

In conclusion, I believe that planting trees on every street is worth the loss of some parking, because the benefits for health and the environment are greater than the inconvenience. The change will be accepted more easily if it is introduced step by step.', 'PUBLISHED');
