There are clear benefits when families plant trees. Each tree gives shade, cleans the air and provides a home for birds and insects. When a family plants a tree near their home, children learn to care for living things and to feel responsible for their neighbourhood. In some towns, families even name their trees, which builds a sense of community.

However, not every family is able to plant a tree. People who live in flats may have no garden, and those who rent their homes may move before the tree is fully grown. A young tree also needs regular watering for several years, which can be difficult for busy parents or elderly people.

For these reasons, local councils should make tree planting easy rather than expecting every family to do it alone. Councils could choose suitable places, provide young trees and organise planting days, while volunteers could help water the trees during their first summers.

In conclusion, families planting trees is a positive idea, but it works best as an invitation supported by local government rather than a duty for everyone.', 'PUBLISHED');
